Philipp Lucas <phlucas@online-club.de> writes:

> My first ascension was genocideless; since then I make sure to
> genocide at least mind flayers and liches.

Nowadays I don't usually bother genociding anything except in
emergencies, like in the case of a tourist who fell to the Castle and
met a master lich without magic resistance, a cursed scrolls of
teleportation, or a reliable engraving method, and too far from
upstairs.

However, when I do start genociding, then my genocides go in this
order:

Certainly:
  black dragons
  umber hulks

Usually:
  master mind flayers

If I have lots of extra genocide scrolls:
  xorns
  yellow dragons
  ochre jellies
  green slime

Can someone guess why this list and why in this order? Also, all
golems used to be in the list but it seems that they are nowadays not
genocidable anymore. 

> Going wishless emphasises luck.

For me it would emphasize more patience. Going wishless is quite low
in my list of interesting conducts since spending time for finding the
really essential items is quite boring. I play for fun. Sometimes it
is fun to try some difficult ascension, sometimes it is fun to start a
valkyrie or wizard and just bash everything using all
bordering-on-abuse tactics.

The next conduct that I'll try is a non-cannibal culinarist. I almost
managed one already, I had only about 10 different monster types left
to eat (the riders and several baby dragons and naga hatchlings) when
a captain hit me with a cockatrice corpse and I skipped the warnings
hitting Esc repeatedly.

But anyway, in my opinion the dividing line between a skilled nethack
player and a player who is not yet one is that a skilled player can
quite reliably get an "ascension-cabable" game started on most gaming
sessions. With this I mean that the player can sit in front of
computer, start a number of games and get at least one of them to
roughly quest levels before getting up again. 


